WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.359
So let's take a look at some famous people from history who used their different thinking

2
00:00:10.359 --> 00:00:13.500
modes to help them with their problem solving.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:20.120
If you look at that guy right there, he was Salvador Dali, a very well-known surrealist

4
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.719
painter of the 20th century.

5
00:00:22.719 --> 00:00:27.639
He was the very definition of a wild and crazy guy.

6
00:00:27.639 --> 00:00:31.559
You can see him here with his pet ocelot, Babu.

7
00:00:31.559 --> 00:00:36.279
Dali used to have an interesting technique to help him come up with his fantastically

8
00:00:36.279 --> 00:00:38.880
creative surrealist paintings.

9
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:46.040
He'd relax in a chair and let his mind go free, often still vaguely thinking about what

10
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.479
he had previously been focusing on.

11
00:00:48.479 --> 00:00:55.240
He'd have a key in his hand, dangling it just above the floor, and as he would slip

12
00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:00.680
into his dreams, falling asleep, the key would fall from his hand and the clatter would

13
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:07.279
wake him up just in time so he could gather up those diffuse mode connections and ideas

14
00:01:07.279 --> 00:01:14.160
in his mind and off he'd go, back into the focus mode, bringing with him the new connections

15
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.559
he'd made while in the diffuse mode.

16
00:01:17.559 --> 00:01:23.059
Now you might think, well, you know, that's okay for an artist, but what does it have

17
00:01:23.059 --> 00:01:27.620
to do with more scientific or mathematical kinds of thinking?

18
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:35.019
Well, if you look down here, this guy was Thomas Edison, one of the most brilliant inventors

19
00:01:35.019 --> 00:01:36.019
ever.

20
00:01:36.019 --> 00:01:43.160
According to legend, what Edison used to do was he'd sit and relax in his chair, holding

21
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.379
ball bearings in his hand.

22
00:01:45.379 --> 00:01:52.900
He'd relax away, letting his mind run free, although it would often noodle back in a much

23
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:57.540
more relaxed way to what he'd been focusing on previously.

24
00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:03.940
When Edison would fall asleep, the ball bearings would drop and clatter to the ground, just

25
00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:10.139
as with Thalle, and it would wake Edison up and off he'd go, with his ideas from the

26
00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:15.539
diffuse mode, ready to take them into the focus mode and build on them.

27
00:02:15.539 --> 00:02:20.500
So the bottom line is, when you're learning something new, especially something that's

28
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:27.979
a little more difficult, your mind needs to be able to go back and forth between the

29
00:02:27.979 --> 00:02:30.779
two different learning modes.

30
00:02:30.779 --> 00:02:33.419
That's what helps you learn effectively.

31
00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:39.899
You might think of it as a bit analogous to building your strength by lifting weights.

32
00:02:39.899 --> 00:02:44.899
You would never plan to compete in a weightlifting competition by waiting until the very day

33
00:02:44.899 --> 00:02:50.100
before a meet and then spending that entire day working out like a fiend.

34
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.699
I mean, it just doesn't happen that way.

35
00:02:53.699 --> 00:03:01.059
To gain muscular structure, you need to do a little work every day, gradually allowing

36
00:03:01.059 --> 00:03:03.220
your muscles to grow.

37
00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:11.380
Similarly, to build neural structure, you need to do a little work every day, gradually

38
00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:19.220
allowing yourself to grow a neural scaffold to hang your thinking on, a little bit every

39
00:03:19.339 --> 00:03:20.339
day.

40
00:03:20.339 --> 00:03:22.419
And that's the trick.

41
00:03:22.419 --> 00:03:28.100
In summary then, we also learned that analogies provide powerful techniques for learning.

42
00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:34.460
We learned about how the brain's two different thinking modes, focused and diffuse, each

43
00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:38.699
helps us learn, but in very different ways.

44
00:03:38.699 --> 00:03:43.979
And finally, we learned that learning something difficult can take time.

45
00:03:43.979 --> 00:03:49.860
The brain needs to alternate its ways of learning as it grapples with and assimilates

46
00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.020
the new material.

47
00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.580
Thanks for learning about learning.

48
00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:55.940
I'm Barbara Oakley.